  :adr/title "ai-gftd-cybersecurity and kotoba-lang/opencloud: verified against a wire-compatible server, not against a live cloud"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-07-30"
  :adr/context
  "gftdcojp needed a CNAPP/EPP replacement (Wiz, Trend Micro). The owner chose detection-engine-first and said explicitly that cloud credentials are not to be arranged yet (「まだ用意しない」), so every claim had to be earnable without touching a real account. That constraint produced the second repository: kotoba-lang/opencloud, a wire-compatible AWS/Azure/GCP/Kubernetes/OCI/OSV server, so a client could be checked against protocol behaviour rather than against recorded responses it was written to satisfy."
  :adr/decision
  "Two repositories, both private, both created and pushed on 2026-07-30 (they had existed only as untracked directories until then). Neither is registered in manifest/west.yml or manifest/fleet-db.edn — see :adr/consequences.

  ai-gftd-cybersecurity (gftdcojp, main 028b4e1): portable .cljc engines for advisory matching, OCI image inventory, cloud posture, CIEM and attack paths; a read-only agent; a Cloudflare Worker fleet plane (roster, schedule, work queue, alerting).

  kotoba-lang/opencloud (main a3c5bf3): eight faces speaking the real protocols, whose value is the refusals — throttling in each provider's envelope, sessions that expire mid-scan, an ExternalId refused identically to an untrusted role, blobs redirected to a host that rejects the registry token, replicas that contradict the listing that named a bucket, namespace-scoped credentials that cannot list across a cluster.

  Both declare their own limits in machine-checked data: resources/capabilities.edn (what is implemented and how far it was checked) and resources/surfaces.edn (every operation, every fidelity claim with the test that holds it, and every stated absence)."
  :adr/consequences
  "Verified state on 2026-07-30, every suite run individually rather than through the aggregate script, which now exceeds ten minutes: cybersecurity 358 JVM tests / 2046 assertions, 117/117 differential cases agreeing across JVM and Node, agent 39, access 8, collector 6, gateway 19, fleet 26, cloud-scans 28, conformance 99. opencloud 75 tests / 391 assertions, 17/17 portability. Zero failures.

  Declared state: 27 capabilities, 25 implemented, live-service verification 0, 41 known gaps. 8 surfaces, 48 operations, 43 fidelity claims, 20 stated absences.

  NOT DONE, and the named next step: neither repository is in manifest/west.yml or manifest/fleet-db.edn. Registration was left undone deliberately — the superproject checkout is 190 commits behind origin/main, and a manifest write from a stale checkout is how pins regress silently (see ADR-2607022900). Registration needs a synced superproject and the server-side single-entry commit path.

  The boundary that has not moved: no live AWS, Azure, GCP, Kubernetes, registry or osv.dev has been called, no real D1 has been written, the Worker cron has not run on Cloudflare, and nothing wakes the agent. capabilities.edn carries each of those as a gap rather than as prose."
  :adr/body
  "## What the two repositories are for

  One shape of defect drove the whole design: **record a failure, then return success.** A collector that could not read a source, a feed that failed to load, a layer that could not be decompressed, a bucket whose policy was refused — each produced the same empty answer a clean estate produces. So every collector states what it attempted, what it got, what it could not read, and what it never looked at, and an empty result is reported as clean only when something measured.

  The emulator exists because unit tests on recorded responses cannot produce the failures that matter. Defects it found that fixtures had not: a registry client forwarding its credential across a blob redirect to object storage; a throttle read as a gap; a region fan-out counting global services twice; an STS fallback to the caller's own account; a session renewal signed with the expired session; an Ubuntu image queried as `Debian:22.04`, so the most-used base image in the world came back clean every time; a `403 AccessDenied` on a bucket's policy status substituted with an empty PolicyStatus, reporting the one bucket nobody could see as not public.

  ## Claims, and making them checkable

  Late in the work the pattern shifted from covering absences to checking claims that nothing verified. Each of these was asserted in prose and is now machine-checked:

  - the read-only allowlist — and the claim had gone false: `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` was added on a path that must be unsigned, so it reached AWS without passing the guard and was on no allowlist at all
  - the write gate — tested for one driver and assumed for the other nine; now 10 drivers x 4 refusal conditions, plus the membership of the approval-requiring set, because demoting a mode is a one-word change that removes the gate
  - the agent-root path boundary — six of seven task-supplied paths were bounded; `:advisories-path` reached the loader directly, so a task could name any file on the host and the error reported whether it existed
  - `patch/execute` — took the whole argument vector from the task, so an approved task ran `apt-get remove -y openssh-server` under a driver named for patching. An approver reads a task, not a list of everything the driver could do
  - `credentials are never persisted` — now canary-checked on every connector, on the refused path as well as the working one, because a secret escapes through the error message that echoes the request
  - unobserved promise rejections — the agent reports its result on stderr, so an abandoned sibling promise printed a stack trace after it. All three agent suites now run under --unhandled-rejections=strict
  - the numbers this documentation quotes — they had drifted, both understating what was unverified, in the document whose purpose is to say what has not been checked

  ## What is honest about the coverage

  A client that passes against opencloud has been checked against what surfaces.edn describes and nothing else. The 20 stated absences are the useful number: read-after-write, CloudTrail, session policies, OIDC verification, AAD, management groups, Resource Graph, OAuth, folders and organizations, organization policies, per-API quotas, watch, admission, SubjectAccessReview, registry push, credential minting from a cloud role, uncompressed layers, ecosystem archives, ECR token scope, ECR repository operations.

  Two things were found to be sound rather than broken, and are recorded as such: VEX suppression matches precisely (a different package, distribution or version suppresses nothing), and no credential reaches a result on any of eight paths.

  One cross-product claim was found false and left measured rather than fixed: the attack-graph projection uses ai-gftd-kabuto's vocabulary and cannot be transacted into kabuto — it keys a topology of node types on a keyword id while this emits one node per resource keyed by its provider id, plus nine attributes kabuto does not define. Which side should move is a decision across two products, so `posture/projection-errors` measures the difference against kabuto's own schema instead."}]
